<!-- FORMULATION COMPONENTS FOR AEROSOL DELIVERY -->
<section class="section" id="formulation-components-for-aerosol-delivery">
  <h2>Formulation Components for Aerosol Delivery</h2>
  <p>The transition from a bulk lubricant to an aerosol-dispensed product requires a sophisticated understanding of solvent dynamics and propellant interaction. The lubricant must be diluted with a volatile carrier solvent to achieve the low viscosity necessary for atomization; however, this solvent must evaporate rapidly after application to leave behind the high-viscosity, high-adhesion film. If the solvent evaporates too slowly, the lubricant may drip off before it can bond; if it evaporates too quickly, the lubricant may not have enough time to penetrate the internal pins.</p>
  <p>
                The transition from a bulk lubricant to an aerosol-dispensed product requires a sophisticated understanding of solvent dynamics and propellant interaction. The lubricant must be diluted with a volatile carrier solvent to achieve the low viscosity necessary for atomization; however, this solvent must evaporate rapidly after application to leave behind the high-viscosity, high-adhesion film. If the solvent evaporates too slowly, the lubricant may drip off before it can bond; if it evaporates too quickly, the lubricant may not have enough time to penetrate the internal pins.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Solvent Carriers:</strong> Regulating the "Open Time" for maximum internal penetration.</li>
                <li><strong>Propellant Systems:</strong> LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas) provides consistent pressure, while DME (Dimethyl Ether) offers unique solvent properties.</li>
                <li><strong>Stability Agents:</strong> Ensuring that solid lubricants (like MoS2) do not settle or clog the aerosol valve.</li>
                <li><strong>Safety Standards:</strong> Utilizing non-chlorinated solvents to comply with environmental and health regulations.</li>
            </ul>
</section>

<!-- INDUSTRIAL APPLICATIONS AND PERFORMANCE STANDARDS -->
<section class="section" id="industrial-applications-and-performance-standards">
  <h2>Industrial Applications and Performance Standards</h2>
  <p>Performance testing is essential to validate that the aerosol lubricant meets the rigorous demands of industrial environments. Standardized tests, such as the Four-Ball Wear Test (ASTM D4172) and the Falex Pin and Vee Block Test, are used to quantify the lubricant's ability to resist scuffing and wear. Additionally, salt spray testing (ASTM B117) ensures that the protective film provides adequate corrosion resistance for outdoor or maritime applications.</p>
  <p>
                Performance testing is essential to validate that the aerosol lubricant meets the rigorous demands of industrial environments. Standardized tests, such as the Four-Ball Wear Test (ASTM D4172) and the Falex Pin and Vee Block Test, are used to quantify the lubricant's ability to resist scuffing and wear. Additionally, salt spray testing (ASTM B117) ensures that the protective film provides adequate corrosion resistance for outdoor or maritime applications.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>ASTM D4172:</strong> Measuring the wear scar diameter under controlled loads.</li>
                <li><strong>Salt Spray Testing:</strong> Critical for chains used in marine or winter road environments.</li>
                <li><strong>Spin-Off Testing:</strong> Measuring the percentage of lubricant lost at specific RPMs.</li>
                <li><strong>Penetration Rating:</strong> Assessing the fluid's ability to move through 0.05mm clearances.</li>
            </ul>

